Test 12 fails because I asserted identity for `openReview` from atReview, but `atReview` screen stopped, review null; `openReview` returns a new object `{...state, screen:'review'}` only if review?.result. Wait code:
```
case openReview:
  if (state.screen !== 'stopped') return state;
  if (!state.review?.result) return state;
  return {...};
```
atReview has review null, so returns `state` identity. But actual in failure is a new object; wait test line 164? The failure shows expected/actual same structure but not reference-equal; likely first assertion `flowReducer(review, {openStopped}).screen`? No, deep? Let's see test source line numbering. The failing line is in compiled; line 171. In source:
```
assert.equal(flowReducer(review, { type: 'openStopped' }).screen, 'stopped');
assert.equal(flowReducer(atReview(), { type: 'openReview' }), atReview());
```
The second compares two separate calls to `atReview()` — different objects! My mistake: `atReview()` creates new state each call. Use `const stopped = atReview(); assert.equal(flowReducer(stopped,...), stopped)`. Fix.

Also note test 12 line: yes.

